  "hints": [
    "An empty plot is plantable exactly when its left neighbour and its right neighbour are both free; a missing neighbour counts as free.",
    "You do not need to rewrite the array. Carry one flag for whether the plot you just left is now occupied, and remember to set it when you plant.",
    "Once n flowers are placed you can answer immediately, and n = 0 is trivially satisfiable."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force refuses to commit to a choice. It walks all 2^choose subsets implicitly with a top-down search: at plot i it takes the better of skipping (the next state inherits whether plot i is occupied) and planting when plot i is empty, its left neighbour is free and its right neighbour is free. Memoizing on (index, previous plot occupied) collapses the exponential tree to n states, each doing only a couple of comparisons and a memo lookup; without the memo this search is O(2^n), which is why it is not the intended solution. The optimal scan makes the same decision but commits greedily left to right with two scalars: whether the previous plot ends up occupied and whether the next plot is empty.",
    "why_optimal": "Planting at the leftmost legal empty plot can only ever conflict with the single plot to its right, so exchanging any optimal solution's first planted plot for the leftmost legal one never reduces the total; the greedy pass therefore plants the true maximum. Every plot has to be inspected once, so O(n) time with O(1) working memory is optimal for n = flowerbed.length up to 100.",
    "pitfalls": "Reading flowerbed[i - 1] without guarding i = 0, or flowerbed[i + 1] at the last index; both neighbours that do not exist count as free. Forgetting that a plant you just chose occupies the next plot, which is why the flag matters more than the untouched input array. Returning false for n = 0, where no planting is needed and the answer is always true."
  },
